{"body": "The conjecture did not misread the rule. It correctly read \u201cThe object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\u201d as requiring {\"b\": 2} for Reply A, and it predicted the code would instead return {\"c\": 3}. The machine returned `({'c': 3}, ())` for the input ` ```json\n{\"a\": 1} {\"b\": 2}\n```\n{\"c\": 3}`, confirming the prediction. So the rule as written and the code part company: the fenced candidate is the whole span between fences, `{\"a\": 1} {\"b\": 2}`, which fails to parse as one object, so the code falls through to the top-level pool and takes the last top-level object, `{\"c\": 3}`. A next conjecture should test a fenced block containing exactly one valid JSON object followed by a top-level object, e.g. ` ```json\n{\"a\": 1}\n```\n{\"c\": 3}`, and conjecture that the check returns `{\"a\": 1}` because the fenced candidate parses and the rule says the last object inside the fence is scored. It should avoid repeating the two-adjacent-objects-inside-a-fence setup, since that already established the fallback behavior."}